r all $n \\ge 1$.\n4. Therefore, by the comparison test, $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n + 5}$ is divergent.\n\nWhich of the following sequences could be used for $b_{n}$ in steps 2 and 3 to make the argument valid?\n\n1. $b_{n} = \\frac{1}{6n}$\n2. $b_{n} = \\frac{1}{21n}$\n3. $b_{n} = \\frac{1}{n}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "067e54ca-4f01-5199-8215-1696fbf0c529" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Suitable Series For the Comparison Test For Convergence" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049073 :topic/math-academy-id 745 :topic/title "The Comparison Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c1a64df-c6b3-51a3-ab44-e2dd3377ab03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081468} {:answer/id #uuid "10bc1c15-1adb-50f9-8f0e-d1ce20203633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081469} {:answer/id #uuid "176d2e49-9827-5712-811a-8fef991810ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081470} {:answer/id #uuid "61d4ba1d-85e0-542e-a6e5-356b5d36e026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081471} {:answer/id #uuid "65bdcbad-9b45-5703-b893-2ae5e53eb341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081472}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3e87fea5-bf7a-59c9-a9bd-1dc4401db310" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081467}] :question/id #uuid "9200d9fe-8aa0-5e99-9760-994d17da7d89" :question/math-academy-id "q-50669" :question/problem "Consider the following argument regarding the convergence of the series $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{2^{n} + 1}$.\n\n1. Let $a_{n} = \\frac{1}{2^{n} + 1}$. Note that $a_{n} > 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$, so we can use the comparison test.\n2. The series $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}$ is convergent, and $b_{n} > 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$.\n3. $a_{n} \\le b_{n}$, for all $n \\ge 1$.\n4. Therefore, by the comparison test, $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{2^{n} + 1}$ is convergent.\n\nWhich of the following sequences could be used for $b_{n}$ in steps 2 and 3 to make the argument valid?\n\n1. $b_{n} = \\frac{1}{2^{n}}$\n2. $b_{n} = \\frac{5}{2^{n}}$\n3. $b_{n} = \\frac{1}{5^{n}}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "067e54ca-4f01-5199-8215-1696fbf0c529" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Suitable Series For the Comparison Test For Convergence" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049073 :topic/math-academy-id 745 :topic/title "The Comparison Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "868cf7bb-4366-53ae-a9e2-7157561689ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081474} {:answer/id #uuid "070fb577-f4b7-5112-833c-10b00ba4cf87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081475} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc9cefe-b79e-596c-9769-027172314efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081476} {:answer/id #uuid "700f764c-3569-5471-adb3-63e4ea950c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081477} {:answer/id #uuid "7a177c51-42d8-5ada-9695-373ef742fb34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081478}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7b807e51-8e45-5d23-ae62-7f3ff2e876ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081473}] :question/id #uuid "56a6b0ae-e6b0-53c8-b94c-c8754f470cb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-50670" :question/problem "Consider the following argument regarding the convergence of the series $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{3}{5^{n} + 1}$.\n\n1. Let $a_{n} = \\frac{3}{5^{n} + 1}$. Note that $a_{n} > 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$, so we can use the comparison test.\n2. The series $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}$ is convergent, and $b_{n} > 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$.\n3. $a_{n} \\le b_{n}$, for all $n \\ge 1$.\n4. Therefore, by the comparison test, $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{3}{5^{n} + 1}$ is convergent.\n\nWhich of the following sequences could be used for $b_{n}$ in steps 2 and 3 to make the argument valid?\n\n1. $b_{n} = \\frac{3}{2^{n}}$\n2. $b_{n} = \\frac{3}{5^{n}}$\n3. $b_{n} = \\frac{3}{n^{2}}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083301 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb43fd2b-f6c4-51e6-870b-3f87193a5905" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent Series by Applying the Alternating Series Test" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050417 :topic/math-academy-id 747 :topic/title "The Alternating Series Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f3144cd-389b-5349-afba-707a946964af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081480} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e6eb44-339a-5d6a-9142-caae14b15858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081481} {:answer/id #uuid "db48508e-023e-5c36-bc25-5d8953e5f58c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id