uestion/math-academy-id "q-154690" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f299f0d25bfee9303a6485413d44f1a6ce7b863c79ccae91f4f8d5d604f2bac3.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{4},\\, & 0\\le x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4)$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. $f(x)$ is negative for some values of $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x-1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}x-1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\frac{16}{2}-4]-[\\frac{0}{2}-0]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement I is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f78a76-4d2f-5694-971c-e734bc330602" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular Polygons" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050514 :topic/math-academy-id 1322 :topic/title "Regular Polygons"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb2cd303-4f8d-5cc2-b910-57894fbbad5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102337} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaa5122-d981-5a3b-bccd-fcba6